.TH SERVE 2
.SH NAME
post, serve \- post a service and answer it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "post :: proc(path: string) -> (serve_fd: int, err: i64)"
.B "serve :: proc(fd: int, handler: vectra9.Handler, state: rawptr, frame: []u8, out: []u8, payload: []u8, remove_stops := true) -> (served: u64, why: Serve_End)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Post
makes a pipe, creates
.I path
and writes the decimal digits of one end's descriptor into it, then
closes both descriptors it spent.
What it answers is the other end, ready for
.IR serve .
On any failure along the way, everything already opened is closed again,
and
.I err
is the kernel's errno for the step that refused.
.PP
.I Serve
answers 9P requests read from
.I fd
until something ends the loop.
It reads a frame into
.IR frame ,
decodes it, calls
.I handler
with
.I state
as its own argument and
.I payload
as the storage a reply may build its bytes in, encodes the reply into
.IR out ,
and writes it back.
.I Frame
and
.I out
must each hold a whole message; their size is the msize this server
honours, and the handler's
.B Tversion
reply should say no more.
.PP
.I Handler
is a
.BR vectra9.Handler ,
described in
.IR vectra9 (2).
It is the same signature every kernel server implements, so a handler
cannot tell whether it answers from ring 0 behind a transport or from
ring 3 behind this pipe.
.PP
.I Serve
answers one request at a time, on the caller's own stack.
A handler that cannot answer immediately has nothing to hold the request
with; a server whose reads must park uses
.IR 9p (2)
instead, on the same handler.
.PP
A server stops two ways, both reported in
.IR why .
A
.B Tremove
answered and then obeyed is
.BR .Removed ,
the client's own request to stop; the reply goes out before the loop
returns, so the remover hears yes.
.I Remove_stops
is
.B true
by default, which fits a fixed tree; a filesystem that removes a file
rather than stopping on one passes
.BR false .
The pipe ending is
.BR .Hangup .
A frame no loop can read past, either a size beyond
.I frame
or bytes that will not decode, is
.BR .Broken .
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Ramfs (4)
posts and serves in three lines:
.IP
.EX
fd, perr := libuser.post("/srv/ramfs")
if perr < 0 {
	libuser.exit(0x71)
}
_, why := libuser.serve(fd, handler, nil, frame_in[:], frame_out[:], payload[:])
switch why {
case .Removed:
	libuser.exit(0)
case .Hangup:
	libuser.exit(0x68)
case .Broken:
	libuser.exit(0x72)
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/serve.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR fid (2),
.IR 9p (2),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR bind (2),
.IR ramfs (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Post
answers a negative
.I err
naming the step that failed: the pipe, the create, or the write of the
descriptor's digits.
.I Serve
never answers an error itself; a malformed frame ends the loop with
.B .Broken
rather than being reported to the handler.
